1
00:00:02,560 --> 00:00:04,374
It's time to talk about the thing that I've

2
00:00:04,374 --> 00:00:07,200
been dancing around for all the lessons so
far, the harness itself.

3
00:00:07,200 --> 00:00:09,559
This is the layer that allows your LLM that
is

4
00:00:09,559 --> 00:00:13,200
effectively just generating text to actually
do things in your systems.

5
00:00:13,200 --> 00:00:16,035
When we did the simple demo earlier with the
bash tool call,

6
00:00:16,035 --> 00:00:17,453
it was the start of a harness,

7
00:00:17,453 --> 00:00:20,383
but obviously modern harnesses are way more
complex than that,

8
00:00:20,383 --> 00:00:23,360
offering functionality that goes far beyond
writing bash calls.

9
00:00:23,360 --> 00:00:26,000
Modern harnesses have fancy memory systems
built in.

10
00:00:26,000 --> 00:00:29,648
They have tools that allow them to spawn
additional sub-agents or their own threads

11
00:00:29,648 --> 00:00:31,670
or sometimes complex workflows where they
have

12
00:00:31,670 --> 00:00:34,000
multiple steps that agents run through
independently.

13
00:00:34,000 --> 00:00:36,547
They have their own tools for editing files,
reading files,

14
00:00:36,547 --> 00:00:39,440
updating data through MCP calls, and a whole
of these other things.

15
00:00:39,440 --> 00:00:42,969
There are a ton of different harnesses that
have tons of different capabilities,

16
00:00:42,969 --> 00:00:45,528
and I could spend a lot of time going
through all of that,

17
00:00:45,528 --> 00:00:48,175
but the harsh reality is that by the time
this video is out,

18
00:00:48,175 --> 00:00:50,160
they probably will have changed
meaningfully.

19
00:00:50,160 --> 00:00:55,040
So I'm gonna once again skirt around all of
this and teach you durable skills instead.

20
00:00:55,040 --> 00:00:57,446
So we're going to ignore that top because
we're

21
00:00:57,446 --> 00:01:00,160
talking about how to not get trapped by your
harness.

22
00:01:00,160 --> 00:01:01,760
A much more important thing.

23
00:01:01,760 --> 00:01:03,360
Tell me if you've been there before.

24
00:01:03,360 --> 00:01:06,560
You finally start getting really deep into
cursor.

25
00:01:06,560 --> 00:01:09,803
You've set up all the rules, you have
everything in a state you like,

26
00:01:09,803 --> 00:01:12,341
you're making changes to your code pretty
effectively,

27
00:01:12,341 --> 00:01:14,880
and then Claude Code drops and everybody
swears by it.

28
00:01:14,880 --> 00:01:16,710
Or maybe you've already made the switch over

29
00:01:16,710 --> 00:01:18,790
to Claude Code and you're getting pretty
familiar,

30
00:01:18,790 --> 00:01:21,120
but your bill's going up a bunch and you
just heard that

31
00:01:21,120 --> 00:01:23,782
the new OpenAI model is really good and it's
way cheaper to run,

32
00:01:23,782 --> 00:01:26,444
so your workplace is trying to push you to
switch over to Codex,

33
00:01:26,444 --> 00:01:28,400
but you just set everything up for Claude
Code.

34
00:01:28,400 --> 00:01:30,000
You're screwed now, right?

35
00:01:30,000 --> 00:01:31,440
Well, in some ways, yes.

36
00:01:31,440 --> 00:01:35,443
It's a lot easier to change models than it
is to change harnesses, unless, of course,

37
00:01:35,443 --> 00:01:38,880
the model is being provided subsidized but
exclusively through 1 harness.

38
00:01:38,880 --> 00:01:41,120
Anthropic is anthropic, they'll be how they
are.

39
00:01:41,120 --> 00:01:41,630
But ideally,

40
00:01:41,630 --> 00:01:45,200
you should be able to switch between these
things without feeling too much friction.

41
00:01:45,200 --> 00:01:48,240
Not to self-plug too hard, but I love
***'s commentary here.

42
00:01:48,240 --> 00:01:50,508
If you're not familiar with him, he's the
creator of Effect,

43
00:01:50,508 --> 00:01:53,040
which is a really popular way to make your
TypeScript scale better.

44
00:01:53,040 --> 00:01:55,933
He posted recently saying that since he
started using T3 code,

45
00:01:55,933 --> 00:01:58,080
he no longer cares what harness is being
used.

46
00:01:58,080 --> 00:01:59,920
He'd actually like to get rid of all of
them.

47
00:01:59,920 --> 00:02:02,240
This is the thing that you should strive
towards.

48
00:02:02,240 --> 00:02:05,040
You should not really feel the harness while
you work.

49
00:02:05,040 --> 00:02:08,217
Fixating on which harness you're using to me
feels similar

50
00:02:08,217 --> 00:02:11,504
to fixating really deeply on which code
editor you're using,

51
00:02:11,504 --> 00:02:15,120
but it's even less useful and less
productive than in those cases.

52
00:02:15,120 --> 00:02:18,023
Generally speaking, the labs are now
training their models

53
00:02:18,023 --> 00:02:20,625
on their harness through their real-world
histories,

54
00:02:20,625 --> 00:02:23,127
so most of the major releases like from
Claude and

55
00:02:23,127 --> 00:02:26,480
OpenAI are gonna behave best in Claude Code
and Codex respectively.

56
00:02:26,480 --> 00:02:30,649
Generally speaking, if you're leaving those,
you should have good reasons for it,

57
00:02:30,649 --> 00:02:33,120
but you also shouldn't care too much in
general.

58
00:02:33,120 --> 00:02:35,172
I find people, myself included,

59
00:02:35,172 --> 00:02:40,400
get way too fixated on using whatever
harness is coolest and hottest right now.

60
00:02:40,400 --> 00:02:43,806
But what matters much more is that you're
taking the most advantage of

61
00:02:43,806 --> 00:02:47,552
the tools you are using today in building
skills not like the markdown files,

62
00:02:47,552 --> 00:02:49,693
but the actual skills around working with AI

63
00:02:49,693 --> 00:02:52,320
that will be durable across these different
solutions.

64
00:02:52,320 --> 00:02:55,280
There's a lot of things that go into the
harness.

65
00:02:55,280 --> 00:02:58,720
You have the UI that is exposing the
capabilities of the harness.

66
00:02:58,720 --> 00:03:02,712
This could be a nice desktop app like the
Codex app or the Claude Code desktop app,

67
00:03:02,712 --> 00:03:06,800
or it could be a terminal UI like an open
code in the terminal or the Claude command.

68
00:03:06,800 --> 00:03:09,638
And the user, which is, believe it or not,
you,

69
00:03:09,638 --> 00:03:13,321
sits in front and sends things and
interfaces through the UI,

70
00:03:13,321 --> 00:03:15,253
which interfaces to the harness,

71
00:03:15,253 --> 00:03:19,600
which then goes off and hits the APIs for
whatever models you are using.

72
00:03:19,600 --> 00:03:23,040
And then that generates tokens that get sent
back to the harness.

73
00:03:23,040 --> 00:03:25,920
And if the tokens match certain patterns, it
triggers certain behaviors.

74
00:03:25,920 --> 00:03:28,310
And then eventually it spits those outputs
into the

75
00:03:28,310 --> 00:03:30,560
UI that you read and then make decisions
around.

76
00:03:30,560 --> 00:03:35,330
But there are other things that go into the
harness, capabilities, knowledge,
connections,

77
00:03:35,330 --> 00:03:39,040
and more, like maybe you have an MCP server
that is connected to this.

78
00:03:39,040 --> 00:03:41,783
And now the harness has to inform the APIs
and

79
00:03:41,783 --> 00:03:45,360
the LLMs of this MCP's capabilities and that
it's available.

80
00:03:45,360 --> 00:03:49,996
Maybe you have something that automatically
runs a get status command right before it

81
00:03:49,996 --> 00:03:53,760
hits up the API so that the model knows
immediately what is going on.

82
00:03:53,760 --> 00:03:55,636
This is why I had that silly thing in the
last

83
00:03:55,636 --> 00:03:57,920
lesson where the model was aware of the
files I deleted.

84
00:03:57,920 --> 00:04:00,240
That was my mistake because I'd forgotten.

85
00:04:00,240 --> 00:04:03,286
Claude Code loves to include the git status
in whatever

86
00:04:03,286 --> 00:04:06,000
thing it sends as soon as you start a new
thread.

87
00:04:06,000 --> 00:04:08,693
The problem that I see a lot is that people
think

88
00:04:08,693 --> 00:04:12,320
of the harness not as this little box that
everything connects to.

89
00:04:12,320 --> 00:04:16,410
They think of it like this, this whole
thing, all of the commands it can run,

90
00:04:16,410 --> 00:04:20,765
all of the stuff built into the system
prompt, the MCPs that you're connecting to,

91
00:04:20,765 --> 00:04:25,280
the skills that you wrote that it has access
to, the UI that you're using it through.

92
00:04:25,280 --> 00:04:26,720
They see all of this as the same.

93
00:04:26,720 --> 00:04:29,200
And I don't blame them for thinking that
way, because that's what the labs want.

94
00:04:29,200 --> 00:04:34,037
Anthropic and OpenAI really wants you to
think that this is Claude or Codex or

95
00:04:34,037 --> 00:04:36,952
whatever else because then you'll get locked
in

96
00:04:36,952 --> 00:04:39,680
and never leave the thing that you're using.

97
00:04:39,680 --> 00:04:44,760
But similar to what *** said on Twitter,
I don't even feel or notice the harness at

98
00:04:44,760 --> 00:04:47,680
this point because I work in a different UI
layer.

99
00:04:47,680 --> 00:04:49,600
Personally, I'm using T3 code.

100
00:04:49,600 --> 00:04:52,720
There's plenty of others too, like herder,
human layer, orca, and more.

101
00:04:52,720 --> 00:04:56,824
Obviously, I like my own and it's open
source, so you can change it however you
want,

102
00:04:56,824 --> 00:05:00,880
but there's tons of different options for
how you will interface with the harnesses.

103
00:05:00,880 --> 00:05:04,720
And then when you switch from 1 harness to
another, less of your workflow will break.

104
00:05:04,720 --> 00:05:05,351
Personally,

105
00:05:05,351 --> 00:05:10,400
I think 1 of the first things you should do
is yank that UI layer away from the harness.

106
00:05:10,400 --> 00:05:14,727
If you continuously tie yourself to the
specific way of working that the harness
has,

107
00:05:14,727 --> 00:05:17,018
then you're going to be screwed the next
time

108
00:05:17,018 --> 00:05:19,360
the best model switches from 1 lab to
another.

109
00:05:19,360 --> 00:05:22,800
Right now, as the time of filming, there is
no question that the best models are

110
00:05:22,800 --> 00:05:25,294
currently coming from anthropic if you're
working on code,

111
00:05:25,294 --> 00:05:26,800
but that could change at any point.

112
00:05:26,800 --> 00:05:27,539
And when it does,

113
00:05:27,539 --> 00:05:30,756
if you're all built around Claude Code and
you want to move over to codex,

114
00:05:30,756 --> 00:05:34,625
you're going to have to relearn everything
from your key bindings to where you should
put

115
00:05:34,625 --> 00:05:38,320
your skills to how the model behaves when it
connects to things or does computer use.

116
00:05:38,320 --> 00:05:41,146
So the more you can abstract the layer that
you interface with,

117
00:05:41,146 --> 00:05:43,120
the less scary it is when a new model drops.

118
00:05:43,120 --> 00:05:46,720
This gets particularly dangerous once you
start customizing the harness.

119
00:05:46,720 --> 00:05:51,844
I've seen people do some crazy things with
Claude Code, building it up into this big,

120
00:05:51,844 --> 00:05:55,280
beautiful, chaotic machine that has endless
capabilities.

121
00:05:55,280 --> 00:05:58,480
Every time I see them do that, it ends up
burning them some amount.

122
00:05:58,480 --> 00:05:59,126
For example,

123
00:05:59,126 --> 00:06:03,920
Matt Pocock went all in customizing Claude
Code to make it everything he wanted and
more.

124
00:06:03,920 --> 00:06:07,680
And then Anthropic put out Claude mods that
even further extended your customization.

125
00:06:07,680 --> 00:06:11,553
He has since almost entirely moved away
because he's so frustrated with the way that

126
00:06:11,553 --> 00:06:13,950
Claude Code subscriptions don't have clear
terms for

127
00:06:13,950 --> 00:06:15,840
where you can and can't use those tokens.

128
00:06:15,840 --> 00:06:18,535
And he doesn't know what and how to build
around

129
00:06:18,535 --> 00:06:22,240
Claude because of how weird anthropics been
with those same terms.

130
00:06:22,240 --> 00:06:26,231
Obviously open source solutions are going to
be less uh risky in this way,

131
00:06:26,231 --> 00:06:28,928
but generally you should try to build in
ways that

132
00:06:28,928 --> 00:06:31,840
aren't so tied to a specific harness and its
features.

133
00:06:31,840 --> 00:06:35,760
If you're okay with throwing it all away
it's totally fine and actually quite fun.

134
00:06:35,760 --> 00:06:38,545
I love building weird hacks into Claude
Code, Codex, Pi,

135
00:06:38,545 --> 00:06:40,597
whatever else I'm using at any given time,

136
00:06:40,597 --> 00:06:44,800
but I have found that I'm way more
productive when I do as little of that as
possible.

137
00:06:44,800 --> 00:06:47,080
I switch to a UI or application that I'm

138
00:06:47,080 --> 00:06:49,931
very familiar with using and have all my
keybinds,

139
00:06:49,931 --> 00:06:54,720
all of my skills, all of the things I rely
on for my workflow there and ready to go.

140
00:06:54,720 --> 00:06:55,864
And from that point,

141
00:06:55,864 --> 00:06:59,869
I just kind of fire things off and can get a
good gut feel really fast

142
00:06:59,869 --> 00:07:04,160
as to what tools I do and don't like because
I have a flow that works well.

143
00:07:04,160 --> 00:07:07,280
And in the end, we're just sending text to
these things anyways.

144
00:07:07,280 --> 00:07:10,838
We're writing prose in English for what we
want and we're hoping that the

145
00:07:10,838 --> 00:07:14,640
model has access to the tools and knowledge
it needs to make the right result.

146
00:07:14,800 --> 00:07:16,958
Similar to what I was pushing on the last
lesson,

147
00:07:16,958 --> 00:07:19,689
your goal shouldn't be to make sure your
environment has every

148
00:07:19,689 --> 00:07:22,640
single thing you could ever need before you
send your first prompt.

149
00:07:22,640 --> 00:07:24,080
You should hit the ground running.

150
00:07:24,080 --> 00:07:27,120
Try to use these things and get work done
with them.

151
00:07:27,120 --> 00:07:31,040
And as you run into problems, that's when
you should start making adjustments.

152
00:07:31,040 --> 00:07:31,374
Ideally,

153
00:07:31,374 --> 00:07:34,800
you should make those adjustments in ways
that are easy to reproduce other places.

154
00:07:34,800 --> 00:07:38,880
1 of the things agents are great at is
recreating and rewriting stuff in different
shapes.

155
00:07:38,880 --> 00:07:42,044
If you have skills that are formatted in a
way that only cursor likes,

156
00:07:42,044 --> 00:07:45,298
or maybe you have some old cursor rule files
that you want to port over,

157
00:07:45,298 --> 00:07:46,880
you can tell your agent to do that.

158
00:07:46,880 --> 00:07:49,200
They do need somewhere where it all lives.

159
00:07:49,200 --> 00:07:51,200
Everybody has their own strategies for this.

160
00:07:51,200 --> 00:07:53,680
I quite like mine, so I'm going to show that
to you.

161
00:07:53,680 --> 00:07:57,200
This is a real repo that I maintain actively
called Fleet.

162
00:07:57,200 --> 00:08:01,576
I have it called Fleet because it manages
all of my skills, my config,

163
00:08:01,576 --> 00:08:04,640
my MCPs and everything across all of my
machines.

164
00:08:04,640 --> 00:08:05,920
And I have a lot of them now.

165
00:08:05,920 --> 00:08:09,105
I have like 16 computers that I'm using for
my agent work on a given day,

166
00:08:09,105 --> 00:08:12,640
and I pass things around to the different
boxes depending on what I'm working on.

167
00:08:12,640 --> 00:08:15,918
I've started working on automatically
hitting the right box for the right thing,

168
00:08:15,918 --> 00:08:19,360
but uh that's a lesson for another time,
just some fun stuff I'm experimenting with.

169
00:08:19,360 --> 00:08:22,160
What we're here for is to see how I'm
organizing things.

170
00:08:22,160 --> 00:08:25,866
And I want to be clear, I don't actually
open this folder up a whole

171
00:08:25,866 --> 00:08:28,482
lot outside of going to make changes to the
prose

172
00:08:28,482 --> 00:08:31,316
in my skills and markdown files because
that's where

173
00:08:31,316 --> 00:08:33,440
a lot of the value I can bring here is.

174
00:08:33,440 --> 00:08:35,680
The rest is just the general management.

175
00:08:35,680 --> 00:08:37,904
So I have here skills as a folder and I

176
00:08:37,904 --> 00:08:41,440
have different subfolders for ones that I
only want in Claude.

177
00:08:41,440 --> 00:08:43,520
Why would I have skills I only want in
Claude?

178
00:08:43,520 --> 00:08:46,748
That's because I had a few skills in here
that I only wanted to have

179
00:08:46,748 --> 00:08:50,640
used when I was using Claude because they
were teaching Claude how to call Codex.

180
00:08:50,720 --> 00:08:53,200
These ones actually don't exist anymore, so
I can delete those folders.

181
00:08:53,200 --> 00:08:54,880
As you can tell, I don't spend a lot of time
here.

182
00:08:54,880 --> 00:08:57,531
But the Anthropic front end design skill is
1 that works really

183
00:08:57,531 --> 00:09:00,560
well with anthropics models that I found
doesn't work great with others.

184
00:09:00,560 --> 00:09:05,266
So I put it in this folder and I have a
metadata field here for the harness where

185
00:09:05,266 --> 00:09:10,320
I say only Claude and now this skill will be
on my Claude Code instances across my fleet.

186
00:09:10,320 --> 00:09:15,360
I then have a universal folder for all of
the skills I want in multiple different
places.

187
00:09:15,360 --> 00:09:18,560
So this 1, full send, I use multiple times a
day.

188
00:09:18,560 --> 00:09:20,696
This is the skill I invoke when I know the
model can

189
00:09:20,696 --> 00:09:23,120
complete the task and I don't want to have
to see it again.

190
00:09:23,120 --> 00:09:24,640
I just want it to go merge the change.

191
00:09:24,640 --> 00:09:28,781
And here I specify that the harnesses I want
it to be in are Claude, Codex, Anti-Gravity,

192
00:09:28,781 --> 00:09:29,200
and Muse.

193
00:09:29,200 --> 00:09:32,080
And I want this on both my Macs, which is
Darwin, and on my Linux machines,

194
00:09:32,080 --> 00:09:33,962
because I do have some skills that are only
on my

195
00:09:33,962 --> 00:09:35,920
Macs and then some that are only on my Linux
boxes.

196
00:09:35,920 --> 00:09:38,960
How am I managing and configuring all of
this?

197
00:09:38,960 --> 00:09:39,840
Great question.

198
00:09:39,840 --> 00:09:42,240
You can probably guess the answer if you
know me particularly well.

199
00:09:42,240 --> 00:09:45,015
I open a new thread in the fleet repo and I
tell

200
00:09:45,015 --> 00:09:48,080
it what I changed or what I want and then it
does it.

201
00:09:48,080 --> 00:09:51,755
If I make a new skill, I tell it I made a
new skill, it will commit it,

202
00:09:51,755 --> 00:09:56,000
push it up to the repo, and then push it to
all the right places across the fleet.

203
00:09:56,000 --> 00:09:58,480
I didn't write any custom scripts to do this
for me.

204
00:09:58,480 --> 00:10:00,160
I don't even know if there are any right
now.

205
00:10:00,160 --> 00:10:03,460
I vaguely recall at 1 point that Astra wrote
a script to

206
00:10:03,460 --> 00:10:07,879
automate this process of distributing my
skills and config across my fleet,

207
00:10:07,879 --> 00:10:10,000
but I'm not actually sure if it did.

208
00:10:10,000 --> 00:10:11,520
I just think it did.

209
00:10:11,520 --> 00:10:15,328
I don't care though, because when I tell it
I want the skill on all the machines,

210
00:10:15,328 --> 00:10:16,080
it just does it.

211
00:10:16,080 --> 00:10:17,464
And when I get a new machine,

212
00:10:17,464 --> 00:10:21,520
I open up this project and I say configure
the new machine based on what I have here.

213
00:10:21,520 --> 00:10:25,040
And this isn't just for configuring my
harness and giving it all the skills it
needs.

214
00:10:25,040 --> 00:10:28,553
This goes as far as describing how I like to
use my computers,

215
00:10:28,553 --> 00:10:33,199
what tools they should have installed on
them, how Tailscale should be configured,

216
00:10:33,199 --> 00:10:37,675
how to identify given machines, the color
coding systems that are built for it,

217
00:10:37,675 --> 00:10:40,621
all these things are just files in this
project that

218
00:10:40,621 --> 00:10:43,680
I use as a reference point when I configure
new tools.

219
00:10:43,680 --> 00:10:46,307
But even then, when I try out something
truly new,

220
00:10:46,307 --> 00:10:50,353
like I move over to anti-gravity because I'm
trying out the new Google model,

221
00:10:50,353 --> 00:10:54,400
I will start without any of this initially
just to see how it works, vanilla.

222
00:10:54,400 --> 00:10:57,602
And then after I've gotten an idea for how
it works by itself,

223
00:10:57,602 --> 00:10:59,719
I then tell Claude or Codex to go through

224
00:10:59,719 --> 00:11:02,560
this repo and configure everything the way
I'm used to.

225
00:11:02,560 --> 00:11:03,942
I then try that out for a bit,

226
00:11:03,942 --> 00:11:07,166
I figure out the gaps between what I liked
before and what I like now,

227
00:11:07,166 --> 00:11:10,160
and then I microadjust accordingly until I
land somewhere I like.

228
00:11:10,160 --> 00:11:13,643
That said, I do have some workflows that I
really like using right now,

229
00:11:13,643 --> 00:11:15,213
so I've encoded those as skills,

230
00:11:15,213 --> 00:11:18,941
and as soon as I'm working on a machine or a
harness that doesn't have them,

231
00:11:18,941 --> 00:11:22,080
I usually go pretty mad and I'll just tell
it to copy them over.

232
00:11:22,080 --> 00:11:26,560
All of this combined has made me feel pretty
much entirely harness agnostic.

233
00:11:26,560 --> 00:11:31,403
I was able to move over fully from Claude
Code to Muse in like 3 minutes, not even,

234
00:11:31,403 --> 00:11:36,480
and barely noticed the difference in terms
of how it behaved and what it had access to.

235
00:11:36,480 --> 00:11:39,760
And at that point, I'm effectively just
evaluating the model.

236
00:11:39,760 --> 00:11:44,160
The harness you're using barely matters
outside of the system prompt at this point.

237
00:11:44,160 --> 00:11:47,068
And I found that adjusting that will have a
much bigger impact

238
00:11:47,068 --> 00:11:50,960
in your day-to-day experience than changing
which tools are available to the model.

239
00:11:50,960 --> 00:11:53,322
I find that if you get too deep in 1
specific harness,

240
00:11:53,322 --> 00:11:57,040
you're gonna lose out on a lot of the things
that make all of this stuff really cool.

241
00:11:57,040 --> 00:11:59,120
Think about the composition of your team at
work.

242
00:11:59,120 --> 00:12:03,120
Imagine if you took the best engineer and
you cloned them 5 times.

243
00:12:03,120 --> 00:12:05,360
How much more code can you write?

244
00:12:05,360 --> 00:12:06,240
Probably a lot.

245
00:12:06,240 --> 00:12:08,720
But how much better are your code reviews
gonna get?

246
00:12:08,720 --> 00:12:12,088
Probably not that much, because if 5 people
think the exact same way,

247
00:12:12,088 --> 00:12:14,480
they're gonna come to the exact same
conclusions.

248
00:12:14,480 --> 00:12:17,200
Ideally the tools you're using will let you
hop between different brains.

249
00:12:17,200 --> 00:12:20,880
So different models, different agents,
different harnesses, different whatever.

250
00:12:20,880 --> 00:12:23,600
And this is 1 of the things that I'm really
proud of in T3 code.

251
00:12:23,600 --> 00:12:26,400
We allow for your agents to call other
harnesses.

252
00:12:26,400 --> 00:12:31,235
So here we can see that this run I'm doing
with Opus 5.5 is also able to call Muse

253
00:12:31,235 --> 00:12:34,891
for writing code sometimes and it's even
calling GPT-61Soul in

254
00:12:34,891 --> 00:12:37,840
Codex to get feedback on the work that it's
doing.

255
00:12:37,840 --> 00:12:41,391
I find that this is useful even if a smart
model is asking for feedback from a dumber

256
00:12:41,391 --> 00:12:43,521
1 because the dumber model might think about
things

257
00:12:43,521 --> 00:12:46,320
slightly differently and find things that
the smarter model missed.

258
00:12:46,320 --> 00:12:49,360
And if I was all in on Claude Code, I
wouldn't be able to do this.

259
00:12:49,360 --> 00:12:51,515
In fact, when I was all in on Claude Code,

260
00:12:51,515 --> 00:12:56,080
I had a bunch of modifications in it so that
I could use OpenAI models in my Claude Code.

261
00:12:56,080 --> 00:12:57,311
If you were curious,

262
00:12:57,311 --> 00:13:01,680
I still have this set up and I can in Claude
Code call my codex models.

263
00:13:01,680 --> 00:13:05,077
I did this for a while, but I found that the
codex models behave best

264
00:13:05,077 --> 00:13:07,637
in codex and the cloud models behave best in
Claude,

265
00:13:07,637 --> 00:13:10,935
so I now have a solution that lets me use
Claude models with Claude

266
00:13:10,935 --> 00:13:15,120
and Codex models with Codex without the
friction of them not being able to
interface.

267
00:13:15,120 --> 00:13:17,484
I used to have them generate markdown files
or HTML

268
00:13:17,484 --> 00:13:19,709
pages so I could link the context between
the 2,

269
00:13:19,709 --> 00:13:21,840
and that ended up becoming my job for a
while.

270
00:13:21,840 --> 00:13:25,120
It's effectively just passing the context
from 1 agent to another,

271
00:13:25,120 --> 00:13:28,400
putting it in my code review tools or
wherever else it needs to go

272
00:13:28,400 --> 00:13:31,680
so that I could get the best out of all of
these different things.

273
00:13:31,680 --> 00:13:35,440
And then I have tools like macroscope,
greptile, and code rabbit reviewing PRs.

274
00:13:35,440 --> 00:13:39,120
You're getting 1 layer of feedback there
that prevents agents from making dumb
mistakes.

275
00:13:39,120 --> 00:13:42,579
And now inside of my actual daily driving
ADE, which is T3 code,

276
00:13:42,579 --> 00:13:45,714
I'm able to get additional feedback from
other families of

277
00:13:45,714 --> 00:13:49,120
models and other harnesses without having to
change how I work.

278
00:13:49,120 --> 00:13:52,480
When a new model comes out, it's just
another thing to click in my drop-down.

279
00:13:52,480 --> 00:13:54,320
It's not a whole new world to learn.

280
00:13:54,320 --> 00:13:58,895
It's not like switching frameworks from
React to Svelte or from Svelte to Angular,

281
00:13:58,895 --> 00:14:00,960
it's more like switching a CSS class.

282
00:14:00,960 --> 00:14:04,574
You're just changing over from 1 flavor of
LLMs to another,

283
00:14:04,574 --> 00:14:08,800
and the harness starts to evaporate once you
think about it this way.

284
00:14:08,800 --> 00:14:12,160
What's going on now is effectively another
level of abstraction.

285
00:14:12,160 --> 00:14:16,443
Similar to how back in the day we would
abstract from the binary code that

286
00:14:16,443 --> 00:14:18,874
our computers were running up to low-level

287
00:14:18,874 --> 00:14:21,710
languages that were much more writable by
humans,

288
00:14:21,710 --> 00:14:26,224
up to runtimes that would allow for even
simpler languages to be built on top,

289
00:14:26,224 --> 00:14:31,200
that type of abstracting more and more as
things evolve is going on rapidly right now.

290
00:14:31,200 --> 00:14:33,224
So of course at the bottom we have code,

291
00:14:33,224 --> 00:14:36,311
but above that we had LLMs that could start
writing the code,

292
00:14:36,311 --> 00:14:39,600
usually through autocomplete when you were
typing in your editor.

293
00:14:39,600 --> 00:14:43,722
Maybe you would select some text command K
and say change this thing and it would,

294
00:14:43,722 --> 00:14:45,280
but we keep abstracting on top.

295
00:14:45,280 --> 00:14:48,860
Eventually harnesses happened and the role
of the harness was

296
00:14:48,860 --> 00:14:51,970
to give the LLMs access to more things that
you could

297
00:14:51,970 --> 00:14:54,435
then trust it to do more work through a UI

298
00:14:54,435 --> 00:14:57,663
layer like a terminal with claude code or
with T3 code,

299
00:14:57,663 --> 00:15:01,360
whatever else, it is very likely we're going
to abstract again.

300
00:15:01,360 --> 00:15:04,876
I would be surprised if most of us haven't
moved to an assistant

301
00:15:04,876 --> 00:15:08,392
layer of some form on top over the next few
months to few years,

302
00:15:08,392 --> 00:15:11,029
where instead of managing every thread
yourself,

303
00:15:11,029 --> 00:15:15,040
you're talking to a higher level agent that
commands the threads for you.

304
00:15:15,040 --> 00:15:20,320
And this is also why I get so concerned
about people obsessing over any 1 of these
layers.

305
00:15:20,320 --> 00:15:24,221
We've mostly gotten over our obsession with
like the specific framework we're using,

306
00:15:24,221 --> 00:15:27,379
the specific syntax we're writing, fighting
over tabs versus spaces,

307
00:15:27,379 --> 00:15:28,400
those types of things.

308
00:15:28,400 --> 00:15:29,760
That's what we would fight about here.

309
00:15:29,760 --> 00:15:31,972
When we were in the LLMs doing the stuff
era,

310
00:15:31,972 --> 00:15:35,314
we were fighting a lot about which rules to
use, which model to use,

311
00:15:35,314 --> 00:15:37,280
what reasoning efforts and temperatures.

312
00:15:37,280 --> 00:15:39,500
Like I still remember the era where
everybody was

313
00:15:39,500 --> 00:15:42,400
complaining that we didn't have temperature
controls in T3 chat.

314
00:15:42,400 --> 00:15:44,134
This all seemed so important at the time,

315
00:15:44,134 --> 00:15:46,968
but I could tell it wasn't because I knew
the labs would get to the

316
00:15:46,968 --> 00:15:50,480
point where the defaults were right and we
didn't have to tune those knobs anymore.

317
00:15:50,480 --> 00:15:52,240
Now I see the same with harnesses.

318
00:15:52,240 --> 00:15:56,695
Everybody who used to care too much about
what temperature was set on their models,

319
00:15:56,695 --> 00:16:00,560
now cares way too much about which tools are
available in their harness.

320
00:16:00,560 --> 00:16:03,684
I was talking to somebody at an event
yesterday that was trying

321
00:16:03,684 --> 00:16:06,560
to make a startup to help your agents pick
the right tool.

322
00:16:06,560 --> 00:16:07,520
I have bad news for you.

323
00:16:07,680 --> 00:16:09,986
The agents are gonna pick the right tool and
if they didn't,

324
00:16:09,986 --> 00:16:11,600
it's because your tool description sucked.

325
00:16:11,600 --> 00:16:14,000
Stop over-optimizing at this layer.

326
00:16:14,000 --> 00:16:18,240
The harness should be as easy to swap out as
the model you're using is.

327
00:16:18,240 --> 00:16:20,962
And if you are super deep on your 1 specific
harness,

328
00:16:20,962 --> 00:16:24,198
you're going to be screwed when we have
another abstraction and

329
00:16:24,198 --> 00:16:27,280
we move to assistants that are calling our
harnesses for us.

330
00:16:27,280 --> 00:16:29,683
I've only just started playing with this in
T3

331
00:16:29,683 --> 00:16:32,400
code and it's kind of mind-boggling what it
enables.

332
00:16:32,400 --> 00:16:36,929
I know a few people that have used T3 code
way more than me that have done so much more

333
00:16:36,929 --> 00:16:39,480
work and it had tens of thousands of threads
that

334
00:16:39,480 --> 00:16:42,395
did all of it through 1 high message chat
with instinct,

335
00:16:42,395 --> 00:16:44,320
commanding the app remotely for them.

336
00:16:44,320 --> 00:16:47,579
And the more effort you put into super
customizing the harness,

337
00:16:47,579 --> 00:16:50,320
the less prepared you will be when we
abstract again.

338
00:16:50,320 --> 00:16:54,076
So generally speaking, what you want to do
is make the work that your

339
00:16:54,076 --> 00:16:57,614
agents are doing as easy to understand as
possible for the agent,

340
00:16:57,614 --> 00:16:59,520
regardless of what tool they're in.

341
00:16:59,520 --> 00:17:03,467
This could be your agent in CLAUDE.md, but
ideally it's the code base itself,

342
00:17:03,467 --> 00:17:07,773
as I showed before, describing to people and
more importantly to agents where things

343
00:17:07,773 --> 00:17:10,080
are so that they go to the right place
first.

344
00:17:10,080 --> 00:17:12,505
There's a trick I learned with ADHD a long
time

345
00:17:12,505 --> 00:17:15,600
ago from Adam Savage that has fundamentally
changed my life.

346
00:17:15,600 --> 00:17:19,078
When you are trying to find something in
your house and it's not where you

347
00:17:19,078 --> 00:17:22,650
expect it and then you go hunt and hunt
until you find where it actually is,

348
00:17:22,650 --> 00:17:23,920
there's a place it belongs.

349
00:17:23,920 --> 00:17:25,520
It's wherever you checked first.

350
00:17:25,520 --> 00:17:28,240
Always put the thing back where you checked
first.

351
00:17:28,240 --> 00:17:30,560
This applies tenfold to LLMs.

352
00:17:30,560 --> 00:17:35,280
If you make it so the obvious, easy thing it
does is right, you're gonna succeed.

353
00:17:35,280 --> 00:17:39,455
If you add hundreds of fancy tools to the
harness layer so that the model is checking

354
00:17:39,455 --> 00:17:43,581
500 times with all these fancy things you
built into it if the code is right or not,

355
00:17:43,581 --> 00:17:46,480
you're never gonna figure out what the
model's doing wrong.

356
00:17:46,480 --> 00:17:48,202
And you're also probably just gonna
overwhelm

357
00:17:48,202 --> 00:17:50,000
it with choices that don't actually make
sense.

358
00:17:50,000 --> 00:17:52,880
And the most important thing to understand
about these

359
00:17:52,880 --> 00:17:55,280
abstractions is that when we move up a
layer,

360
00:17:55,280 --> 00:17:57,840
the layer below becomes much easier to swap
out.

361
00:17:57,840 --> 00:18:01,280
When we were living in code, changing
frameworks was miserable.

362
00:18:01,280 --> 00:18:03,740
But now that we've abstracted as much as we
have,

363
00:18:03,740 --> 00:18:08,058
LLMs can rewrite a code base in a different
language, much less a different framework,

364
00:18:08,058 --> 00:18:08,560
trivially.

365
00:18:08,560 --> 00:18:10,443
And then once the harnesses got really good,

366
00:18:10,443 --> 00:18:13,995
we got to the point where we can swap out
the model and still get good performance,

367
00:18:13,995 --> 00:18:17,120
even if it's a different harness model
pairing than is normally expected.

368
00:18:17,120 --> 00:18:20,480
And once we're in assistance, we're just not
going to be thinking about this anymore.

369
00:18:20,480 --> 00:18:24,880
And the more abstracted we get, the less the
layer that we were in prior matters.

370
00:18:25,280 --> 00:18:27,818
so the conclusion on my lesson about
harnesses is

371
00:18:27,818 --> 00:18:30,304
to stop thinking so much about them and
build in

372
00:18:30,304 --> 00:18:32,997
a way where the harness you're using doesn't
feel so

373
00:18:32,997 --> 00:18:36,000
important and the way you're working is what
matters most.

374
00:18:36,000 --> 00:18:39,173
And that's why I'm really excited for my
last lesson, workflows,

375
00:18:39,173 --> 00:18:43,387
where I'm going to show you how I actually
take the ways I like to work and teach the

376
00:18:43,387 --> 00:18:47,600
models how to do it so that I can be more
effective in how I want to deliver software
